  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1203 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A surface front continues to push eastward through \nthe Tri-State area early this afternoon.  Clearing ahead of this \nfront all the way northwards towards Highway 14 has allowed \nMLCAPE values to push as high as 500-800 J/KG which will allow \nfor low- topped convection to form. While the risks for any \nsignificant hail/wind are low, some concerns continue for the \npotential of stretching updraft and potential funnel/tornado \ndevelopment in a narrow corridor of the CWA. With 0-3km MLCAPE \nnear 300-500 J/KG, 0-1 km helicity does rise to nearly 150-200 \nM2/S2 depending on the degree of SE surface winds near the \nboundary. Localized VGP values surge past 0.5 M/S2, maximized \nsouth of I-90 towards the MO River and into the Highway 20 \ncorridor of NW Iowa through 5pm. \n\nTONIGHT: The passage of the front, and associated mid-lvl dry slot, \nwill bring an end to the precipitation in most locations. However \nthe aforementioned frontal boundary will keep persistent shower and \nisolated thunderstorms in the far southeastern CWA into the \nearly overnight hours. As low-lvl moisture returns from the NW \novernight, saturation depth may increase enough for occasional \nspits to drizzle by daybreak. NW winds will also increase \nthrough the region behind this boundary with the latest CAMS \nindicating potential for 40 mph gust at times and localized \nhigher gusts in the higher MO River valley areas. \n\nTUESDAY-WEDNESDAY: Weak low-lvl lift persists through Tuesday, but \nI'm not anticipating widespread rain at this point. Soundings \nsuggest widespread low-stratus and even the potential for \noccasional drizzle into the afternoon. By Tuesday evening and \nnight, deeper moisture advects back north as a weak wave pivots \ninto the Mid-Mo River valley bringing persistent and more \nwidespread rain risks into the Tri-state area overnight. \n\nWednesday continues to look like a very cool and wet day as the \nmerger of several upper waves into a larger upper trough over the \nPlains, aids in developing a large TROWAL across Minnesota and into \nthe eastern Dakotas.  Depending on the wobble of the upper trough, \nmostly light precipitation may be near continuous through the day on \nWednesday as breezy to gusty northwest winds continue.  Expect \nminimal temperature rises both days, with highs in the 40s to\nlower 50s likely. \n\nTHURSDAY-SUNDAY: A brief period of dry conditions returns for the \nend of the week as mid-lvl ridging moves into the Plains. Cooler \nCanadian high pressure sinks southward into Friday, allowing the \npotential for a widespread frost to a hard freeze Friday and \nSaturday mornings.\n\nThe next risk for precipitation won't arrive until Saturday \ninto Sunday as a fast moving clipper dives southeast. At this \npoint precipitation may stay in the form of liquid, but with \nH850 temperatures falling below zero there might be a non-zero \nrisk for a few snowflakes if precipitation arrives overnight.",
